1
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:07,420
- Hi, guys.      - Hey, Phoebe.

2
00:00:07,630 --> 00:00:08,920
Hey, oh, how did it go?

3
00:00:09,130 --> 00:00:10,170
Um, not so good.

4
00:00:10,380 --> 00:00:13,170
He walked me to the subway and said, "We should do this again."

5
00:00:13,380 --> 00:00:16,050
- Ouch.     - What? He said, "We should do it again."

6
00:00:16,260 --> 00:00:17,300
That's good, right?

7
00:00:17,510 --> 00:00:20,600
Uh, no. Loosely translated, "We should do this again," means:

8
00:00:20,810 --> 00:00:22,850
"You will never see me naked."

9
00:00:24,060 --> 00:00:25,270
Since when?

10
00:00:25,480 --> 00:00:28,810
Since always. It's, like, dating language.

11
00:00:29,020 --> 00:00:32,230
You know, like, "It's not you," means, "It is you."

12
00:00:33,610 --> 00:00:34,990
"You're such a nice guy," means:

13
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:37,160
"I'm gonna be dating leather-wearing alcoholics...

14
00:00:37,370 --> 00:00:39,780
...and complaining about them to you."

15
00:00:40,620 --> 00:00:43,290
Or, you know, um, "I think we should see other people," means:

16
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:45,000
"Ha, ha, I already am."

17
00:00:46,460 --> 00:00:47,880
And everybody knows this?

18
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:50,040
Oh, yeah. Cushions the blow.

19
00:00:50,250 --> 00:00:52,800
Like when you're a kid and your parents put your dog to sleep...

20
00:00:53,010 --> 00:00:55,420
...and they tell you it went off to live on some farm.

21
00:00:55,630 --> 00:00:58,680
That's funny. No, because, uh, our parents actually did...

22
00:00:58,890 --> 00:01:01,390
...uh, send our dog off to live on a farm.

23
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:04,310
Uh, Ross?

24
00:01:05,140 --> 00:01:08,560
Ha, ha. Hello. The Milner's farm in Connecticut?

25
00:01:08,770 --> 00:01:11,190
The Milners, they had this unbelievable farm.

26
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:13,940
They had horses and rabbits that he could chase.

27
00:01:14,150 --> 00:01:15,990
And it was...

28
00:01:18,570 --> 00:01:20,950
Oh, my God. Chi-Chi.

29
00:02:15,210 --> 00:02:18,090
"So how does it feel knowing you're about to die?"

30
00:02:18,300 --> 00:02:21,640
Warden, in five minutes my pain will be over.

31
00:02:21,850 --> 00:02:26,720
But you'll have to live with the knowledge that you sent an honest man to die.

32
00:02:27,930 --> 00:02:29,640
- Hey, that was really good.     - Yeah?

33
00:02:29,850 --> 00:02:31,190
- Yeah.     - Thanks. Let's keep going.

34
00:02:31,400 --> 00:02:36,230
Okay. "So, what do you want from me, Dimon? Huh?"

35
00:02:38,780 --> 00:02:42,660
I just wanna go back to my cell, because in my cell, I can smoke.

36
00:02:42,870 --> 00:02:44,620
"Smoke away."

37
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:53,960
Heh.

38
00:02:57,880 --> 00:03:01,510
I think this is probably why Dimon smokes in his cell alone.

39
00:03:02,550 --> 00:03:04,390
- What?     - Relax your hand.

40
00:03:04,600 --> 00:03:06,350
Let your wrist go.

41
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:07,600
- Not so much.     - Whoa.

42
00:03:07,810 --> 00:03:09,060
Hey.

43
00:03:10,020 --> 00:03:12,100
All right. Now try taking a puff.

44
00:03:12,560 --> 00:03:13,980
Right.

45
00:03:15,860 --> 00:03:19,230
- Okay, no, give it to me.     - No, no, no. I'm not giving you a cigarette.

46
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:22,780
No, it's fine. It's fine. Look, do you want to get this part or not? Here.

47
00:03:23,570 --> 00:03:26,620
All right. Now, don't think of it as a cigarette.

48
00:03:26,830 --> 00:03:30,830
Think of it as the thing that has been missing from your hand.

49
00:03:31,660 --> 00:03:33,960
When you're holding it, you feel right.

50
00:03:34,170 --> 00:03:36,500
- You feel complete.     - You miss it?

51
00:03:36,710 --> 00:03:38,340
No, not so much.

52
00:03:40,090 --> 00:03:42,340
All right. Now we smoke.

53
00:03:45,090 --> 00:03:47,600
Oh, my God.

54
00:03:51,390 --> 00:03:54,010

55
00:03:55,650 --> 00:03:56,690
No, no, no.

56
00:03:56,900 --> 00:04:00,070
They say it's the same as the distance from the tip of a guy's thumb...

57
00:04:00,280 --> 00:04:01,900
...to the tip of his index finger.

58
00:04:08,530 --> 00:04:10,290
That's ridiculous.

59
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:13,160
Can I use either thumb?

60
00:04:15,210 --> 00:04:17,580
All right. Don't tell me, don't tell me.

61
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:21,260
Decaf cappuccino for Joey.

62
00:04:23,220 --> 00:04:24,970
Coffee, black.

63
00:04:26,090 --> 00:04:27,300
Latte.

64
00:04:29,430 --> 00:04:30,600
And an iced tea.

65
00:04:31,220 --> 00:04:34,230
- I'm getting pretty good at this.     - Excellent. That's very good.

66
00:04:34,440 --> 00:04:35,850
Good for me.

67
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:52,450
- You okay, Phoebe?     - Yeah, no, it's not even worth...

68
00:04:52,660 --> 00:04:54,000
It's my bank.

69
00:04:54,210 --> 00:04:55,460
What did they do to you?

70
00:04:55,670 --> 00:04:57,500
It's nothing, it's just... Okay.

71
00:04:57,710 --> 00:05:00,340
I'm going through my mail, and I open up their monthly "statement."

72
00:05:00,550 --> 00:05:02,000
Easy.

73
00:05:04,130 --> 00:05:06,680
And there's 500 extra dollars in my account.

74
00:05:06,880 --> 00:05:10,140
Oh, Satan's minions at work again.

75
00:05:10,850 --> 00:05:13,310
Yes, because now I have to go down there and deal with them...

76
00:05:13,520 --> 00:05:16,730
- What are you talking about? Keep it.     - It's not mine.

77
00:05:16,940 --> 00:05:19,560
I didn't earn it. If I kept it, it would be like stealing.

78
00:05:19,770 --> 00:05:23,360
Yeah, but if you spent it, it would be like shopping.

79
00:05:24,570 --> 00:05:25,690
Okay. Okay.

80
00:05:25,900 --> 00:05:27,990
Let's say I bought a really great pair of shoes.

81
00:05:28,200 --> 00:05:30,490
Do you know what I'd hear with every step I took?

82
00:05:30,700 --> 00:05:33,120
"Not mine. Not mine. Not mine."

83
00:05:33,330 --> 00:05:36,160
And even if I was happy, okay, and skipping...

84
00:05:36,370 --> 00:05:39,540
...I'd hear, "Not mine. Not mine."

85
00:05:39,750 --> 00:05:41,380
We're with you. We got it.

86
00:05:41,590 --> 00:05:46,630
Okay, I'd never be able to enjoy it. It'd be like this giant karmic debt.

87
00:05:46,840 --> 00:05:49,220
Chandler, what are you doing? Hey.

88
00:05:49,430 --> 00:05:51,260
What are you doing?

89
00:05:54,310 --> 00:05:56,350
Oh, gross.

90
00:05:57,060 --> 00:05:59,390
- What is this?     - I'm smoking. I'm smoking. I'm smoking.

91
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:02,190
I can't believe you. You've been so good for three years.

92
00:06:02,400 --> 00:06:04,980
And this is my reward.

93
00:06:06,280 --> 00:06:09,650
Hold on a second. Just think about what you went through the last time you quit.

94
00:06:09,860 --> 00:06:11,740
Okay, so this time I won't quit.

95
00:06:11,950 --> 00:06:14,450
- Put it out.     - All right. I'm putting it out.

96
00:06:15,750 --> 00:06:17,370
Oh, no.

97
00:06:17,580 --> 00:06:19,540
I can't drink this now.

98
00:06:23,590 --> 00:06:25,670
All right, I'm gonna go change. I've got a date.

99
00:06:25,880 --> 00:06:28,340
Is this Alan again? How's it going?

100
00:06:28,550 --> 00:06:32,680
It's going pretty good, you know? It's nice and we're having fun.

101
00:06:32,890 --> 00:06:34,850
- So when do we get to meet the guy?     - Yeah.

102
00:06:35,060 --> 00:06:36,810
Let's see, today's Monday...

103
00:06:37,020 --> 00:06:38,890
- Never.     - Come on.

104
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:42,100
- No, not after what happened with Steve.     - What are you talking about?

105
00:06:42,310 --> 00:06:45,770
We love Steve. Steve was sexy.

106
00:06:48,690 --> 00:06:49,740
Sorry.

107
00:06:49,950 --> 00:06:53,070
Look, I don't know how I feel about him yet. Give me a chance to figure it out.

108
00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:55,310
Well, then can we meet him?

109
00:06:55,410 --> 00:06:56,450
No.

110
00:06:56,660 --> 00:06:58,250
Sorry.

111
00:07:01,710 --> 00:07:03,170
Why should I let them meet him?

112
00:07:03,380 --> 00:07:06,290
I bring a guy home and within five minutes they're all over him.

113
00:07:06,500 --> 00:07:10,340
I mean, they're like coyotes picking off the weak members of the herd.

114
00:07:11,050 --> 00:07:15,390
Listen, as someone who's seen more than her share of bad beef...

115
00:07:15,600 --> 00:07:19,390
...I'll tell you, that is not such a terrible thing.

116
00:07:19,600 --> 00:07:22,100
Come on, they're your friends. They're looking out after you.

117
00:07:22,310 --> 00:07:25,650
I know, I know. I wish that once I'd bring a guy home they actually liked.

118
00:07:25,860 --> 00:07:29,570
Well, you do realize that the odds of that happening are a little slimmer...

119
00:07:29,780 --> 00:07:31,610
...if they never get to meet the guy.

120
00:07:33,570 --> 00:07:35,120
Let it go, Ross.

121
00:07:35,320 --> 00:07:37,080
Yeah? Well, you didn't know Chi-Chi.

122
00:07:41,250 --> 00:07:42,410
Do you all promise?

123
00:07:42,620 --> 00:07:45,330
- Yeah, we promise. We'll be good.     - We promise.

124
00:07:45,540 --> 00:07:48,460
Chandler, do you promise to be good?

125
00:07:56,050 --> 00:08:00,270
You can come in, but your filter tip little buddy has to stay outside.

126
00:08:06,690 --> 00:08:08,190
Hey, Pheebs.

127
00:08:10,070 --> 00:08:13,240
"Dear Ms. Buffay: Thank you for calling attention to our error.

128
00:08:13,450 --> 00:08:17,570
We have credited your account $500. We're sorry for the inconvenience...

129
00:08:17,780 --> 00:08:20,660
...and hope you'll accept this football phone...

130
00:08:23,920 --> 00:08:25,920
...as our free gift." Do you believe this?

131
00:08:26,130 --> 00:08:29,380
Now I have $1000 and a football phone.

132
00:08:31,050 --> 00:08:33,340
What bank is this?

133
00:08:34,840 --> 00:08:36,380
Okay, it's him.

134
00:08:38,050 --> 00:08:39,100
- Who is it?     - Alan.

135
00:08:39,310 --> 00:08:41,100
Chandler. He's here.

136
00:08:44,230 --> 00:08:46,600
Okay, please be good. Please?

137
00:08:46,810 --> 00:08:48,520
I mean, remember how much you like me.

138
00:08:50,940 --> 00:08:54,490
Hi. Alan, this is everybody. Everybody, this is Alan.

139
00:08:56,110 --> 00:08:58,370
- Hi.     - Hey.

140
00:08:59,530 --> 00:09:02,450
I've heard so much about all you guys.

141
00:09:08,880 --> 00:09:11,170
Thanks, I'll call you tomorrow.

142
00:09:14,260 --> 00:09:15,670
Okay.

143
00:09:15,880 --> 00:09:19,300
Okay, let's let the Alan-bashing begin.

144
00:09:19,510 --> 00:09:21,350
Who's gonna take the first shot?

145
00:09:21,560 --> 00:09:22,720
Hmm?

146
00:09:23,140 --> 00:09:24,520
Come on.

147
00:09:25,430 --> 00:09:26,890
I'll go.

148
00:09:29,560 --> 00:09:31,980
Let's start with the way he kept picking at...

149
00:09:32,190 --> 00:09:33,650
You know, I'm sorry, I can't do this.

150
00:09:33,860 --> 00:09:35,320
Can't do it. We loved him.

151
00:09:35,530 --> 00:09:38,110
- We loved him.     - Wait a minute.

152
00:09:38,320 --> 00:09:40,410
We're talking about someone that I'm going out with?

153
00:09:40,620 --> 00:09:42,410
- Yes.     - And did you notice?

154
00:09:42,620 --> 00:09:43,950
Yeah.

155
00:09:48,500 --> 00:09:51,460
Know what was great? The way his smile was kind of crooked.

156
00:09:51,670 --> 00:09:53,670
Yes, yes. Like the man in the shoe.

157
00:09:54,550 --> 00:09:55,670
What shoe?

158
00:09:57,090 --> 00:09:58,430
From the nursery rhyme.

159
00:09:58,630 --> 00:10:00,890
"There was a crooked man who had a crooked smile...

160
00:10:01,100 --> 00:10:03,720
...who lived in a shoe for a while..."

161
00:10:06,810 --> 00:10:09,020
So I think Alan...

162
00:10:09,230 --> 00:10:10,440
...will become the yardstick...

163
00:10:10,650 --> 00:10:13,400
...against which all future boyfriends will be measured.

164
00:10:13,610 --> 00:10:14,770
What future boyfriends?

165
00:10:14,980 --> 00:10:17,530
No, no. I think this could be, you know, "it."

166
00:10:17,740 --> 00:10:18,820
- Really?     - Oh, yeah.

167
00:10:19,030 --> 00:10:22,490
I'd marry him just for his David Hasselhoff impression alone.

168
00:10:22,700 --> 00:10:25,330
You know I'm gonna be doing that at parties, right?

169
00:10:29,120 --> 00:10:31,630
- You know what I like most about him?     - What?

170
00:10:32,460 --> 00:10:35,670
The way he makes me feel about myself.

171
00:10:38,510 --> 00:10:40,050
Yeah.

172
00:10:53,270 --> 00:10:54,860
Hi.

173
00:10:57,190 --> 00:10:58,360
How was the game?

174
00:10:59,740 --> 00:11:01,110
Well...

175
00:11:01,320 --> 00:11:03,870
- We won!     - We won! Thank you! Yeah.

176
00:11:04,700 --> 00:11:07,620
Fantastic. I have one question: How is that possible?

177
00:11:08,700 --> 00:11:11,000
- Alan.     - He was unbelievable.

178
00:11:11,210 --> 00:11:14,750
He was like that Bugs Bunny cartoon where Bugs is playing all the positions.

179
00:11:14,960 --> 00:11:18,420
But instead of Bugs, it was first base, Alan. Second base, Alan...

180
00:11:22,050 --> 00:11:25,470
I mean, it was like he made us into a team.

181
00:11:25,680 --> 00:11:27,680
Yep. We sure showed those Hasidic jewelers...

182
00:11:27,890 --> 00:11:30,520
- ...a thing or two about softball.     - Nice.

183
00:11:31,850 --> 00:11:33,440
Can I ask you guys a question?

184
00:11:33,650 --> 00:11:36,110
Do you ever think that Alan is maybe sometimes...

185
00:11:36,320 --> 00:11:40,280
- What?     - I don't know. A little too Alan?

186
00:11:40,490 --> 00:11:43,910
Oh, no. That's not possible. You can never be too Alan.

187
00:11:44,120 --> 00:11:48,830
Yeah, it's his innate Alan-ness that we adore.

188
00:11:49,160 --> 00:11:51,370
- I, personally, could have a gallon of Alan.     - Oh!

189
00:11:57,380 --> 00:12:00,210
- Hey, Lizzy.     - Hey, Weird Girl.

190
00:12:00,420 --> 00:12:03,130
- I brought you alphabet soup.     - Did you pick out the vowels?

191
00:12:03,340 --> 00:12:06,340
Yes, but I left in the Y's. Because, you know, "Sometimes Y."

192
00:12:07,140 --> 00:12:08,760
Um, I also have something else for you.

193
00:12:08,970 --> 00:12:10,060
Saltines?

194
00:12:10,270 --> 00:12:12,640
No, but would you like $1000 and a football phone?

195
00:12:13,230 --> 00:12:14,600
What?

196
00:12:15,730 --> 00:12:17,310
Oh, my God.

197
00:12:17,570 --> 00:12:20,190
- Oh, my God. There's really money in here.     - I know.

198
00:12:20,400 --> 00:12:22,110
Weird Girl, what are you doing?

199
00:12:22,320 --> 00:12:24,240
Oh, I want you to have it. I don't want it.

200
00:12:24,950 --> 00:12:27,410
No, no. I have to give you something.

201
00:12:27,620 --> 00:12:29,240
No, it's fine. You don't...

202
00:12:29,450 --> 00:12:31,370
Do you want my tinfoil hat?

203
00:12:31,580 --> 00:12:34,460
No, because you need that. No, it's okay. Thanks.

204
00:12:34,670 --> 00:12:36,790
Please. Let me do something.

205
00:12:37,420 --> 00:12:38,840
Okay, all right. I'll tell you what.

206
00:12:39,040 --> 00:12:41,550
You buy me a soda and then we're even. Okay?

207
00:12:42,510 --> 00:12:43,800
- Okay.     - Okay.

208
00:13:45,820 --> 00:13:47,280
Keep the change.

209
00:13:48,030 --> 00:13:49,070
Thanks, Lizzy.

210
00:13:49,280 --> 00:13:50,450
Sure you don't want a pretzel?

211
00:13:50,660 --> 00:13:53,660
- No, I'm fine. Thanks.     - See you.

212
00:13:59,330 --> 00:14:01,210
Huh.

213
00:14:01,590 --> 00:14:02,920
A thumb?

214
00:14:03,130 --> 00:14:04,670
Ew.

215
00:14:05,050 --> 00:14:07,220
I know, I know. I opened it up and there it was...

216
00:14:07,420 --> 00:14:10,840
...just floating in there, like this tiny little hitchhiker.

217
00:14:12,760 --> 00:14:15,390
Maybe it's a contest, you know? Like "Collect all five."

218
00:14:17,940 --> 00:14:19,140
Does, um, anyone wanna see?

219
00:14:19,350 --> 00:14:20,770
No, thanks.

220
00:14:21,770 --> 00:14:24,230
- Oh, hey, don't do that. Come on.     - Cut that out.

221
00:14:24,440 --> 00:14:25,940
It's worse than the thumb.

222
00:14:27,740 --> 00:14:29,530
Hey, this is so unfair.

223
00:14:29,740 --> 00:14:34,030
- Why is it unfair?     - So I have a flaw. Big deal.

224
00:14:34,240 --> 00:14:36,950
Like Joey's constant knuckle-cracking isn't annoying?

225
00:14:37,160 --> 00:14:39,330
And Ross, with his over-pronouncing every single word.

226
00:14:39,540 --> 00:14:41,670
And Monica, with that snort when she laughs.

227
00:14:41,880 --> 00:14:44,210
I mean, what the hell is that thing?

228
00:14:46,170 --> 00:14:49,470
I accept all those flaws. Why can't you accept me for this?

229
00:14:56,640 --> 00:14:59,890
Does the knuckle-cracking bother everybody, or just him?

230
00:15:01,980 --> 00:15:03,350
Well...

231
00:15:03,810 --> 00:15:05,520
...I could live without it.

232
00:15:06,570 --> 00:15:07,730
Huh.

233
00:15:08,440 --> 00:15:13,110
Well, is it, like, a little annoying? Or is it like when Phoebe chews her hair?

234
00:15:15,950 --> 00:15:19,080
Now, don't listen to him, Pheebs, all right? I think it's endearing.

235
00:15:19,290 --> 00:15:21,540
Oh, you do, do you?

236
00:15:27,590 --> 00:15:30,840
You know, there's nothing wrong with speaking correctly.

237
00:15:31,050 --> 00:15:33,840
Indeed there isn't.

238
00:15:36,010 --> 00:15:37,760
I should really get back to work.

239
00:15:37,970 --> 00:15:41,060
Yeah, otherwise someone might get what they actually ordered.

240
00:15:42,350 --> 00:15:46,690
Oh. The hair comes out and the gloves come off.

241
00:16:02,250 --> 00:16:05,830
Did you ever go out with a guy your friends all really like?

242
00:16:06,210 --> 00:16:07,460
No.

243
00:16:08,920 --> 00:16:12,880
Okay. Well, I'm going out with a guy my friends all really like.

244
00:16:14,340 --> 00:16:16,720
We talking about the coyotes here?

245
00:16:16,930 --> 00:16:20,390
Heh. All right. A cow got through.

246
00:16:21,480 --> 00:16:22,770
Can you believe it?

247
00:16:22,980 --> 00:16:24,560
It's just, you know what?

248
00:16:24,770 --> 00:16:27,730
I just don't feel the thing.

249
00:16:27,940 --> 00:16:30,530
I mean, they feel the thing. I don't feel the thing.

250
00:16:30,780 --> 00:16:33,360
Honey, you should always...

251
00:16:33,570 --> 00:16:35,530
...feel the thing.

252
00:16:38,120 --> 00:16:40,910
Listen, if that's how you feel about the guy, Monica, dump him.

253
00:16:41,120 --> 00:16:43,040
I know, it's just gonna be really hard.

254
00:16:43,250 --> 00:16:45,120
Yeah, he's a big boy. He'll get over it.

255
00:16:45,330 --> 00:16:50,000
No, he'll be fine. It's the other five I'm worried about.

256
00:16:50,800 --> 00:16:52,340
Do you have any respect for your body?

257
00:16:52,550 --> 00:16:54,630
Don't you realize what you're doing to yourself?

258
00:16:54,840 --> 00:16:57,090
Hey, you know, I've had it with you and your cancer...

259
00:16:57,300 --> 00:16:59,100
...and your emphysema and your heart disease.

260
00:16:59,300 --> 00:17:02,930
The bottom line is, smoking is cool, and you know it.

261
00:17:05,440 --> 00:17:06,810
Ahem. Chandler?

262
00:17:07,020 --> 00:17:08,770
It's Alan. He wants to speak to you.

263
00:17:08,980 --> 00:17:10,820
Really? He does?

264
00:17:12,230 --> 00:17:14,400
Hey, buddy. What's up?

265
00:17:16,030 --> 00:17:18,360
Oh, she told you about that, huh?

266
00:17:19,160 --> 00:17:23,160
Well, yeah. I have one now and then. Well, yeah, now.

267
00:17:24,080 --> 00:17:25,870
Well, it's not that bad...

268
00:17:28,130 --> 00:17:29,670
Well, that's true.

269
00:17:32,380 --> 00:17:36,170
Gee, you know, no one's ever put it like that before.

270
00:17:36,590 --> 00:17:38,720
Well, okay. Thanks.

271
00:17:49,100 --> 00:17:50,550
God, he's good.

272
00:17:51,980 --> 00:17:53,400
If only he were a woman.

273
00:17:53,610 --> 00:17:55,400
Yeah.

274
00:18:00,490 --> 00:18:02,120
Oh, Lamb Chop.

275
00:18:02,330 --> 00:18:04,950
How old is that sock?

276
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:09,920
If I had a sock on my hand for 30 years, it'd be talking too.

277
00:18:11,460 --> 00:18:16,050
Okay, I think it's time to change somebody's nicotine patch.

278
00:18:17,420 --> 00:18:18,840
Hey.

279
00:18:19,760 --> 00:18:20,800
Where's Joey?

280
00:18:21,010 --> 00:18:24,140
Joey ate my last stick of gum, so I killed him.

281
00:18:25,770 --> 00:18:27,680
Do you think that was wrong?

282
00:18:29,440 --> 00:18:32,150
- I think he's across the hall.     - Thanks.

283
00:18:32,690 --> 00:18:34,190
There you go.

284
00:18:34,980 --> 00:18:37,240
Ooh, I'm alive with pleasure now.

285
00:18:38,320 --> 00:18:41,070
Hey, Pheebs, you gonna have the rest of that Pop Tart?

286
00:18:43,120 --> 00:18:45,910
Does anyone want the rest of this Pop Tart?

287
00:18:47,500 --> 00:18:48,910
Hey, I might.

288
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:51,330
I'm sorry.

289
00:18:51,920 --> 00:18:55,380
You know, those stupid soda people gave me $7000 for the thumb.

290
00:18:55,590 --> 00:18:57,630
- Oh, my God.     - Seven thousand dollars?

291
00:18:57,840 --> 00:19:00,220
And on my way over here, I stepped in gum.

292
00:19:01,640 --> 00:19:03,850
What is up with the universe?

293
00:19:04,050 --> 00:19:05,140
What's going on?

294
00:19:05,350 --> 00:19:08,180
Nothing. I just think it's nice when we're all here together.

295
00:19:08,390 --> 00:19:11,520
It's even nicer when everyone gets to wear their underwear.

296
00:19:14,440 --> 00:19:17,150
- Uh, Joey...     - Oh.

297
00:19:19,740 --> 00:19:21,950
